  "problem": "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{3-3(1)^{2}}{({1}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3}{{4}^{3}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is $0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$",
